{
  "an5.167:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 5.167 ",
  "an5.167:0.2": "17. Resentment ",
  "an5.167:0.3": "Accusation ",
  "an5.167:1.1": "There Sāriputta addressed the mendicants: ",
  "an5.167:1.2": "“Reverends, a mendicant who wants to accuse another should first establish five things in themselves. ",
  "an5.167:2.1": "What five? ",
  "an5.167:2.2": "I will speak at the right time, not at the wrong time. ",
  "an5.167:2.3": "I will speak truthfully, not falsely. ",
  "an5.167:2.4": "I will speak gently, not harshly. ",
  "an5.167:2.5": "I will speak beneficially, not harmfully. ",
  "an5.167:2.6": "I will speak lovingly, not from secret hate. ",
  "an5.167:2.7": "A mendicant who wants to accuse another should first establish these five things in themselves. ",
  "an5.167:3.1": "Take a case where I see a certain individual being accused at the wrong time, not being disturbed at the right time. They’re accused falsely, not disturbed truthfully. They’re accused harshly, not disturbed gently. They’re accused harmfully, not disturbed beneficially. They’re accused with secret hate, not disturbed lovingly. ",
  "an5.167:4.1": "The mendicant who is accused improperly should be reassured in five ways. ",
  "an5.167:4.2": "‘Venerable, you were accused at the wrong time, not at the right time. There’s no need for you to feel remorse. ",
  "an5.167:4.3": "You were accused falsely, not truthfully. … ",
  "an5.167:4.4": "You were accused harshly, not gently. … ",
  "an5.167:4.5": "You were accused harmfull